# Silent Signals

A quiet, privacy-first web experience for recognizing subtle signs of burnout.

Not therapy.  
Not diagnosis.  
No tracking. No storage. No advice.

Just awareness.

---

## What This Is

Silent Signals is a reflective interface that helps users notice quiet patterns often associated with burnout — without collecting data, storing responses, or labeling emotions.

The experience exists only in the moment.  
Once it’s closed, everything disappears.

---

## What This Is Not

- Not a medical or mental-health tool  
- Not a productivity app  
- Not a data-driven platform  
- Not a substitute for professional help  

Silent Signals avoids scoring, charts, and recommendations by design.

---

## Why I Built This

Many digital wellness tools overwhelm users with tracking, metrics, and advice.
I wanted to explore how **interface design alone** — typography, pacing, motion, and restraint — can create space for self-reflection without pressure or judgment.

This project is an experiment in ethical UX and emotional responsibility.

---

## How It Works

- Users move through a series of gentle reflection prompts
- Responses are never stored or displayed as scores
- The interface subtly adapts its tone through color and motion
- The session ends without history, memory, or output

The UI responds emotionally — not numerically.

---

## Privacy by Design

- No login
- No database
- No cookies
- No localStorage
- No analytics

Everything happens client-side and disappears on refresh.

---

## Accessibility & Care

- Keyboard-only navigation supported
- Visible focus states
- `prefers-reduced-motion` respected
- Clear language without diagnostic terms

---

## Tech Stack

- HTML (semantic & accessible)
- CSS (custom properties, transitions, reduced motion)
- Vanilla JavaScript (state & flow control)

No frameworks — intentionally.

---

## Final Note

This page won’t remember.  
But the user might.
